## Matrix Factorization of $-f$

Let $f = xy - wz \in A = \mathbb{Q}[w,x,y,z]$, so $-f = wz - xy$. Observe that $-f$ is the determinant of a $2\times 2$ matrix:

$$-f = wz - xy = \det\begin{pmatrix} x & w \\ z & y \end{pmatrix}\cdot(-1) + \text{(cofactor structure)}.$$

More directly, we use the classical construction via the adjugate. Define

$$U = \begin{pmatrix} x & w \\ z & y \end{pmatrix}, \qquad V = \begin{pmatrix} -y & w \\ z & -x \end{pmatrix}.$$

**Verification that $UV = -f \cdot I_2$:**

$$UV = \begin{pmatrix} x & w \\ z & y \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} -y & w \\ z & -x \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -xy + wz & xw - wx \\ -zy + yz & zw - xy \end{pmatrix}.$$

Since $A$ is commutative, $xw - wx = 0$ and $-zy + yz = 0$, giving

$$UV = \begin{pmatrix} wz - xy & 0 \\ 0 & wz - xy \end{pmatrix} = (wz - xy)\, I_2 = (-f)\, I_2.$$

**Verification that $VU = -f \cdot I_2$:**

$$VU = \begin{pmatrix} -y & w \\ z & -x \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} x & w \\ z & y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -yx + wz & -yw + wy \\ zx - xz & zw - xy \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} wz - xy & 0 \\ 0 & wz - xy \end{pmatrix} = (-f)\, I_2.$$

**Non-triviality:** The pair $(U, V)$ is non-trivial because neither $U$ nor $V$ is a unit (scalar) times the identity matrix — both contain non-constant polynomial entries of positive degree. Since $f$ is a non-zero-divisor (it is irreducible, hence prime, in the UFD $\mathbb{Q}[w,x,y,z]$), the factorization is reduced in the sense that no entry of $U$ or $V$ is divisible by $f$.

**Conclusion.** The pair

$$\boxed{U = \begin{pmatrix} x & w \\ z & y \end{pmatrix}, \quad V = \begin{pmatrix} -y & w \\ z & -x \end{pmatrix}}$$

is a non-trivial matrix factorization of $-f = wz - xy$ in $A = \mathbb{Q}[w,x,y,z]$, satisfying $UV = VU = -f \cdot I_2$.

